*Update (30.08)*:

* the bank got back to us and informed that the DSA is still being reviewed.
* to avoid the delays and to start the execution of payments, they offered to sign the contract without the DSA, and sign the DSA as an addendum to the contract as soon as their internal review is completed.

Privacy informed the Partner Payments team that proceeding without the DSA cannot be endorsed and would have to be a business decision.

{panel:bgColor=#eae6ff}
Bolt`s Partner Payments team clarified that, in its opinion, temporary going forward without the DSA will still be better for data protection due to the following reasons:

# ❌ *The current situation is worse in terms of data vulnerability than what we would have if we sign and implement the agreement.*
## Currently payouts are being processed
### {color:#bf2600}*manually by the local team,*{color}
### {color:#bf2600}*and from a bank account where the local team has access to (this does not match our standards).*{color}
## This means that the data is living in local team laptops and thus is even more vulnerable than if we would use the new bank API; where data is signed and encrypted.
# (/) *By signing and implementing this agreement, we are improving the data protection even without the Data Sharing Agreement.*
## The proposed bank API will assure that payouts get processed
### {color:#006644}*automatically and directly from our systems without human intervention*{color}
### {color:#006644}*and from a bank account that is exclusively controlled from Commerce in the Bolt HQ in Estonia.*{color}
## This means that the data will be in fact, better protected than if we keep the current process alive.

*Based on the above, Partner Payment team accepts the risk and made the business decision to temporary proceed without a DSA.*
{panel}

Partner Payments and Privacy aligned to follow up with the provider in 2 weeks if feedback on the DSA is not received sooner.

